WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.744
Oxygen does not move from the atmosphere to a
cell in one step.

2
00:00:04.744 --> 00:00:08.290
Ventilation brings fresh gas to the alveoli.

3
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:12.729
Diffusion moves oxygen across the respiratory
membrane.

4
00:00:12.729 --> 00:00:15.752
Hemoglobin carries most of it through blood.

5
00:00:15.752 --> 00:00:21.981
The heart and vessels deliver that blood to
tissues, and another diffusion gradient moves

6
00:00:21.981 --> 00:00:23.316
oxygen into cells.

7
00:00:23.316 --> 00:00:26.556
A problem at any stage can reduce delivery.

8
00:00:26.556 --> 00:00:33.200
This lesson connects partial pressure,
membrane exchange, hemoglobin structure, the

9
00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.515
dissociation curve, loading and unloading,
ventilation-perfusion matching, oxygen

10
00:00:38.515 --> 00:00:41.173
content, and clinical interpretation.

11
00:00:41.173 --> 00:00:46.430
The key distinction is between oxygen
pressure, hemoglobin saturation, oxygen

12
00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.533
content, and tissue delivery.

13
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:51.581
They are related, but they are not the same
measurement.

14
00:00:51.722 --> 00:00:55.689
In a gas mixture, each gas contributes a
partial pressure.

15
00:00:55.689 --> 00:01:01.299
Oxygen moves down its partial-pressure
gradient, not because the body actively pumps

16
00:01:01.299 --> 00:01:03.636
each molecule across a membrane.

17
00:01:03.636 --> 00:01:09.420
In healthy lungs, alveolar oxygen partial
pressure exceeds that of incoming pulmonary

18
00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.794
capillary blood, so oxygen diffuses into
blood.

19
00:01:12.794 --> 00:01:18.501
In systemic tissues, cells consume oxygen,
keeping local oxygen partial pressure lower

20
00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:22.781
than arterial blood, so oxygen diffuses out of
capillaries.

21
00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:28.125
The gradient describes direction, while the
membrane area, thickness, diffusion

22
00:01:28.125 --> 00:01:32.934
properties, and time available influence how
much transfer occurs.

23
00:01:32.934 --> 00:01:38.515
If the gradient narrows or the barrier
thickens, equilibration can become less

24
00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:38.980
complete.

25
00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:45.908
The respiratory membrane places air and blood
extremely close while keeping them in separate

26
00:01:45.908 --> 00:01:46.394
compartments.

27
00:01:46.394 --> 00:01:52.971
Oxygen moves from alveolar gas through a thin
fluid layer, alveolar epithelium, basement

28
00:01:52.971 --> 00:01:58.537
membrane region, capillary endothelium,
plasma, and finally into red blood cells.

29
00:01:58.537 --> 00:02:03.754
Large surface area and small diffusion
distance support rapid exchange.

30
00:02:03.754 --> 00:02:10.084
Fluid accumulation, inflammation, loss of
surface area, or reduced contact time can

31
00:02:10.084 --> 00:02:11.139
impair transfer.

32
00:02:11.139 --> 00:02:17.927
Ventilation must also refresh alveolar gas,
and perfusion must bring blood to the

33
00:02:17.927 --> 00:02:18.971
exchanging surface.

34
00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:24.918
A normal membrane cannot oxygenate blood that
never reaches it, and well-perfused

35
00:02:24.918 --> 00:02:29.379
capillaries cannot help alveoli that receive
no fresh air.

36
00:02:29.498 --> 00:02:35.767
Oxygen is not very soluble in plasma, so
dissolved oxygen alone would carry only a

37
00:02:35.767 --> 00:02:36.603
small amount.

38
00:02:36.603 --> 00:02:43.191
Red blood cells contain hemoglobin, a protein
with four subunits and four iron-containing

39
00:02:43.191 --> 00:02:44.205
heme groups.

40
00:02:44.205 --> 00:02:48.070
Each heme can reversibly bind one oxygen
molecule.

41
00:02:48.070 --> 00:02:54.508
Binding does not permanently consume oxygen;
loading in the lungs and unloading in tissues

42
00:02:54.508 --> 00:02:56.348
depend on local conditions.

43
00:02:56.348 --> 00:03:01.697
The dissolved portion is small but important
because oxygen partial pressure reflects

44
00:03:01.697 --> 00:03:04.371
dissolved molecules and helps drive diffusion.

45
00:03:04.371 --> 00:03:09.358
Hemoglobin-bound oxygen contributes most of
the blood's oxygen content.

46
00:03:09.358 --> 00:03:14.432
This distinction explains why a
partial-pressure measurement and an

47
00:03:14.432 --> 00:03:17.815
oxygen-content measurement answer different
physiological questions.

48
00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:24.436
The oxygen-hemoglobin dissociation curve plots
saturation against oxygen partial pressure.

49
00:03:24.436 --> 00:03:31.115
Its S shape reflects cooperative binding:
binding one oxygen changes hemoglobin in a way

50
00:03:31.115 --> 00:03:35.885
that facilitates additional binding, and
unloading one can facilitate further

51
00:03:35.885 --> 00:03:36.362
unloading.

52
00:03:36.362 --> 00:03:42.356
At higher partial pressures, the curve forms a
plateau, helping preserve high saturation

53
00:03:42.356 --> 00:03:45.584
despite moderate changes in lung oxygen
pressure.

54
00:03:45.584 --> 00:03:51.213
In the steeper middle region, a smaller
pressure decrease releases a larger fraction

55
00:03:51.213 --> 00:03:53.811
of bound oxygen, supporting tissue delivery.

56
00:03:53.811 --> 00:04:00.373
Saturation means the percentage of available
heme sites occupied, not the total amount of

57
00:04:00.373 --> 00:04:01.311
hemoglobin present.

58
00:04:01.311 --> 00:04:07.441
A person can have high saturation but reduced
oxygen content if hemoglobin concentration is

59
00:04:07.441 --> 00:04:07.879
low.

60
00:04:07.994 --> 00:04:12.446
Active tissues produce carbon dioxide, acids,
and heat.

61
00:04:12.446 --> 00:04:18.189
These local changes reduce hemoglobin's
affinity for oxygen and shift the dissociation

62
00:04:18.189 --> 00:04:22.497
relationship toward greater unloading at a
given partial pressure.

63
00:04:22.497 --> 00:04:26.604
A lower pH promotes oxygen release through the
Bohr effect.

64
00:04:26.604 --> 00:04:33.951
Increased temperature and 2,3-BPG also favor
unloading under relevant conditions.

65
00:04:33.951 --> 00:04:40.130
In the lungs, conditions favor loading as
carbon dioxide leaves and oxygen partial

66
00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.081
pressure rises.

67
00:04:41.081 --> 00:04:44.959
A curve shift is not automatically good or
bad.

68
00:04:44.959 --> 00:04:51.278
Reduced affinity can improve tissue unloading
but can make lung loading less complete if

69
00:04:51.278 --> 00:04:53.084
alveolar oxygen is limited.

70
00:04:53.084 --> 00:04:58.454
Interpretation must always specify location
and physiological goal.

71
00:04:58.586 --> 00:05:02.655
Ventilation delivers gas; perfusion delivers
blood.

72
00:05:02.655 --> 00:05:08.747
Their relationship determines whether an
alveolar region can contribute effectively to

73
00:05:08.747 --> 00:05:09.301
exchange.

74
00:05:09.301 --> 00:05:13.255
An alveolus with air but little blood wastes
ventilation.

75
00:05:13.255 --> 00:05:19.148
An alveolus with blood but little fresh air
cannot fully oxygenate that blood.

76
00:05:19.148 --> 00:05:24.975
The lungs use local responses to improve
matching, but disease can create substantial

77
00:05:24.975 --> 00:05:25.423
mismatch.

78
00:05:25.423 --> 00:05:31.792
Thinking in terms of ventilation and perfusion
prevents an oversimplified conclusion that

79
00:05:31.792 --> 00:05:34.977
breathing more always fixes low oxygen.

80
00:05:34.977 --> 00:05:41.326
The limitation may be airflow, blood flow,
diffusion, inspired oxygen, hemoglobin,

81
00:05:41.326 --> 00:05:44.212
cardiac output, or tissue use.

82
00:05:44.212 --> 00:05:48.000
The transport chain must be examined stage by
stage.

83
00:05:48.122 --> 00:05:50.291
Four quantities are often confused.

84
00:05:50.291 --> 00:05:54.895
Oxygen partial pressure reflects dissolved
oxygen and drives diffusion.

85
00:05:54.895 --> 00:05:59.857
Saturation reports the fraction of hemoglobin
binding sites occupied.

86
00:05:59.857 --> 00:06:06.372
Oxygen content depends strongly on how much
hemoglobin is present and how saturated it is,

87
00:06:06.372 --> 00:06:08.543
plus a small dissolved component.

88
00:06:08.543 --> 00:06:15.546
Delivery adds blood flow: arterial oxygen
content must be transported by cardiac output.

89
00:06:15.546 --> 00:06:21.533
In anemia, partial pressure and saturation can
be normal while oxygen content is reduced

90
00:06:21.533 --> 00:06:23.671
because there is less hemoglobin.

91
00:06:23.671 --> 00:06:30.509
In low cardiac output, content may be adequate
but delivery is reduced because less blood

92
00:06:30.509 --> 00:06:32.332
reaches tissue each minute.

93
00:06:32.332 --> 00:06:38.288
A pulse oximeter estimates saturation; it does
not directly measure all four quantities.

94
00:06:38.402 --> 00:06:45.024
During exercise, muscle cells consume more
oxygen and produce more carbon dioxide, acid,

95
00:06:45.024 --> 00:06:46.043
and heat.

96
00:06:46.043 --> 00:06:51.172
Local oxygen partial pressure falls,
increasing the diffusion gradient from

97
00:06:51.172 --> 00:06:53.224
capillary blood into tissue.

98
00:06:53.224 --> 00:06:56.846
Conditions also favor hemoglobin unloading.

99
00:06:56.846 --> 00:07:01.349
Blood flow to active muscle rises, increasing
delivery.

100
00:07:01.349 --> 00:07:06.885
These mechanisms coordinate transport with
demand without requiring each red blood cell

101
00:07:06.885 --> 00:07:09.653
to know which tissue is active.

102
00:07:09.653 --> 00:07:15.826
Venous blood normally retains an oxygen
reserve rather than unloading every molecule.

103
00:07:15.826 --> 00:07:19.742
During greater demand, extraction can
increase.

104
00:07:19.742 --> 00:07:26.615
The example shows why the dissociation curve,
circulation, and tissue metabolism belong in

105
00:07:26.615 --> 00:07:30.316
one model: delivery and unloading adjust
together.

106
00:07:30.458 --> 00:07:34.158
When reasoning about oxygen, ask six linked
questions.

107
00:07:34.158 --> 00:07:36.951
Is fresh gas reaching the alveoli?

108
00:07:36.951 --> 00:07:42.436
Is there a sufficient partial-pressure
gradient and functional respiratory membrane?

109
00:07:42.436 --> 00:07:46.339
Is pulmonary blood flowing through ventilated
regions?

110
00:07:46.339 --> 00:07:50.612
Is enough hemoglobin present, and what
fraction is saturated?

111
00:07:50.612 --> 00:07:54.859
Is cardiac output delivering sufficient oxygen
content?

112
00:07:54.859 --> 00:07:58.341
Are tissues able to receive and use that
oxygen?

113
00:07:58.341 --> 00:08:01.811
Then match each measurement to the question it
can answer.

114
00:08:01.811 --> 00:08:07.512
Partial pressure, saturation, content, and
delivery are not interchangeable.

115
00:08:07.512 --> 00:08:13.617
The full chain explains both normal exercise
and clinical problems more accurately than a

116
00:08:13.617 --> 00:08:14.489
single number.

117
00:08:14.489 --> 00:08:20.452
Oxygen reaches working tissue because physical
gradients, protein binding, and circulation

118
00:08:20.452 --> 00:08:23.163
operate as one transport system.
